L.append(lesson(
 'software-engineering','Software engineering','The urgent thread waits while ordinary work runs','See priority inversion and how priority inheritance lets the lock owner finish.','Concurrency · scheduling',
 '<p>A paramedic needs a medicine cabinet. A cleaner has the key, but an ordinary delivery keeps interrupting the cleaner. The paramedic waits even though the delivery is less urgent.</p><p>In software, a low-priority thread holds a lock needed by a high-priority thread. Medium-priority work keeps preempting the lock owner. This is <strong>priority inversion</strong>. With priority inheritance, the lock owner temporarily inherits the waiter’s higher priority so it can finish and release the lock.</p>',
 '<p>Low L holds the Kafka checkpoint lock for two ticks. High H arrives and blocks. Medium M becomes runnable. Without inheritance, M runs before L, so H waits longer. With inheritance, L is boosted above M, runs its remaining two ticks, unlocks, and H proceeds.</p>',
 {'when':'Use priority-inheritance synchronization when bounded latency matters and a high-priority thread may wait on a lock held by lower-priority work.','example':'A real-time ingestion controller waits for a checkpoint mutex while a background metrics thread owns it and normal compression work is runnable.','decision':'First shorten critical sections and avoid unnecessary shared locks. When real-time scheduling is truly required, use an owner-aware PI mutex supported by the OS/runtime; a counting semaphore has no single owner to boost.'},
 'Switch priority inheritance on and off. Follow who owns the lock and which thread the scheduler runs at each tick.',
 'This deterministic toy has three threads, one lock, fixed work, and no interrupts or nested lock chains. Actual schedulers, runtime safepoints, I/O, cgroups, and non-preemptible regions change latency. Priority inheritance bounds one inversion mechanism; it does not guarantee every deadline.',
 '<label><input id="piOn" type="checkbox"> priority inheritance enabled</label><div id="piWorld" class="world"></div>',
 "function paintPI(){const pi=piOn.checked,seq=pi?['L owns','H blocks','L boosted','L unlocks','H runs']:['L owns','H blocks','M runs','M runs','L resumes'];piWorld.innerHTML='<div class=\"lane\"><strong>time</strong>'+seq.map((_,i)=>'<div class=\"slot\">t'+i+'</div>').join('')+'</div><div class=\"lane\"><strong>CPU</strong>'+seq.map((x,i)=>'<div class=\"slot '+(x.startsWith('H')?'ok':x.startsWith('M')?'wait':'')+'\">'+x+'</div>').join('')+'</div><p><strong>What to notice:</strong> '+(pi?'L borrows H’s priority, releases at t3, and H runs at t4.':'M delays the lock owner; H is still blocked at t4.')+'</p>';};piOn.onchange=paintPI;paintPI();",
 [q('Who causes H to wait directly?',['L, because L owns the lock','M, because M owns the lock','The queue'],0,'H blocks on the lock held by L.'),q('Why does M make the inversion worse?',['M preempts L, delaying unlock','M gives H the lock','M raises L automatically'],0,'Medium work delays the low-priority owner even though high-priority work waits.'),q('What does priority inheritance do?',['Permanently raises every thread','Temporarily boosts the lock owner','Removes all locks'],1,'The owner borrows the waiter’s priority until it can release the contested lock.')],
 [{'code':"priority={'L':1,'M':2,'H':3}\nlock_owner='L'",'note':'Low-priority L starts while owning the needed lock.'},{'code':"blocked={'H':'lock'}",'note':'High-priority H arrives but cannot acquire the lock.'},{'code':"effective=dict(priority)\neffective[lock_owner]=priority['H']",'note':'Priority inheritance boosts the owner to H’s priority.'},{'code':"next_thread=max(('L','M'),key=lambda t:effective[t])",'note':'The scheduler chooses boosted L so it can unlock.'}],{'next_thread':'L'},
 [['Linux futex(2): priority-inheritance futexes','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/futex.2.html','Linux man-pages 6.16, September 9, 2026'],['Linux RT-mutex implementation design','https://docs.kernel.org/locking/rt-mutex-design.html','Linux kernel documentation'],['Linux lock types and priority inheritance limits','https://docs.kernel.org/locking/locktypes.html','Linux kernel documentation']],
 'The scheduler lane separates lock ownership from CPU choice: H waits on L, while M delays L only when the owner is not boosted.',
 ['Where could a high-priority control path in your platform wait on background work?','Which measurement would show a long lock-owner delay rather than CPU saturation?']))

L.append(lesson(
 'distinguished-engineer','Distinguished Engineer','Stop starting six migrations; finish two','Use a portfolio work-in-progress limit to turn scattered effort into completed evidence.','Technical strategy · flow',
 '<p>Six half-built bridges do not move any traffic. A platform portfolio can look busy the same way: six migrations are “in progress,” but every team waits for reviews, security decisions, or scarce platform engineers.</p><p>A work-in-progress (WIP) limit caps how many initiatives may actively consume the constrained team. New work waits until one item is finished or explicitly stopped. The purpose is flow and learning—not keeping people idle.</p>',
 '<p>Two platform engineers can complete two small migrations every two weeks. Starting six at once spreads attention across all six; after two weeks, each is roughly one-third complete and no decommission evidence exists. With WIP=2, two finish, produce cost and risk evidence, then the next two start.</p>',
 {'when':'Use a portfolio WIP limit when many “priority” migrations compete for the same reviewers, platform team, or release windows and completion dates keep slipping.','example':'Six Kafka-to-managed-stream migrations share two platform specialists and one security review lane.','decision':'Find the real constraint, set a visible limit at that point, define “done,” and force an explicit start/stop choice. Do not hide urgent incidents or make the number a productivity target.'},
 'Choose the WIP limit. The board shows completed migrations after two timeboxes and how many remain merely started.',
 'This arithmetic model assumes equal-sized work, one bottleneck, no blocked dependencies, and two units of capacity per timebox. Real portfolios need item slicing, variability, expedite policies, and outcome measures. A WIP limit reveals tradeoffs; it does not choose strategy for you.',
 '<label>Active migration limit <input id="wipLimit" type="range" min="1" max="6" value="2"></label><div id="wipWorld" class="world"></div>',
 "function paintWip(){const w=+wipLimit.value,capacity=2,done=Math.min(6,Math.floor(capacity/w)*w),started=Math.min(6,w),waiting=6-started;wipWorld.innerHTML='<div class=\"board\"><div class=\"column\"><strong>Waiting</strong>'+Array.from({length:waiting},(_,i)=>'<div class=cardx>M'+(started+i+1)+'</div>').join('')+'</div><div class=\"column\"><strong>Active · limit '+w+'</strong>'+Array.from({length:Math.max(0,started-done)},(_,i)=>'<div class=\"cardx wait\">M'+(i+1)+' partial</div>').join('')+'</div><div class=\"column ok\"><strong>Done after one box</strong>'+Array.from({length:done},(_,i)=>'<div class=cardx>M'+(i+1)+' evidence</div>').join('')+'</div></div><p>'+(done?'Completed evidence exists for '+done+' migration(s).':'Capacity is spread too thin; nothing reaches done.')+'</p>';};wipLimit.oninput=paintWip;paintWip();",
 [q('What is the primary purpose of a WIP limit?',['Keep everyone maximally busy','Improve flow and finish evidence','Ban all urgent work'],1,'Finishing creates feedback and frees the bottleneck.'),q('Six equal items share capacity for two. With WIP=6 in this toy, what finishes in one box?',['Six','Two','None'],2,'Capacity is spread across all six, so none crosses the finish line.'),q('What must accompany a WIP limit?',['A clear definition of done and visible constraint','More hidden projects','Individual utilization targets'],0,'The policy needs a completion boundary and the bottleneck it protects.')],
 [{'code':'items=6\ncapacity_per_box=2','note':'Model six equal migrations and capacity for two completions.'},{'code':'wip_limit=2','note':'Admit only two migrations into active work.'},{'code':'completed=min(items,capacity_per_box)','note':'The active pair reaches done in one timebox.'},{'code':'evidence_ready=completed\nremaining=items-completed','note':'Completion creates two evidence points for the next decision.'}],{'evidence_ready':2},
 [['DORA value stream mapping guide','https://dora.dev/guides/value-stream-management/','Updated November 13, 2024'],['Google Cloud DevOps capabilities: work in process limits','https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/devops','Living documentation'],['DORA continuous delivery capability','https://dora.dev/capabilities/continuous-delivery/','Living documentation']],
 'The board answers “what actually finishes?” rather than “how many initiatives are active.” Moving the limit changes the distribution of waiting, partial, and evidence-producing work.',
 ['Which team or approval lane is the true constraint across your current portfolio?','What two initiatives would you stop starting so one important migration can finish?']))

L.append(lesson(
 'genai-engineering','GenAI engineering','Approve this exact tool call—not every future call','Bind human approval to the inspected call and revalidate immediately before execution.','Harness engineering · human in the loop',
 '<p>A manager signs a purchase order for 10 laptops. That signature should not authorize a later order for 100 servers just because both use the “buy” system.</p><p>An agent approval should name the tool call the person inspected: tool, parsed arguments, call identity, and relevant policy context. Resume the same paused run state. Before execution, re-run input guardrails because conditions may have changed while the human was deciding.</p>',
 '<p>The agent proposes <code>cancelOrder({orderId:42})</code>. The UI displays order 42 and stores approval for call <code>call_A</code>. A later proposal <code>cancelOrder({orderId:99})</code> has call <code>call_B</code>. It needs a new decision. A sticky “always approve this tool” choice is broader and should be rare, explicit, and policy-bounded.</p>',
 {'when':'Use call-specific approval for tools that spend money, delete data, change access, message people, or otherwise create hard-to-reverse effects.','example':'A support agent may cancel one customer order after a human reviews the exact order ID and reason.','decision':'Render parsed arguments and impact, approve/reject the pending item on the same run state, then revalidate authorization and policy just before execution. Avoid sticky approval for arguments whose risk varies.'},
 'Approve call A, then change the proposed order. The receipt stays bound to A and the new call remains blocked.',
 'The browser uses a simple call-ID and argument fingerprint. It does not run the OpenAI Agents SDK, authenticate the reviewer, protect serialized state, prevent UI spoofing, or solve concurrent state changes. Cryptographic hashing alone does not establish authorization; execution-side checks remain required.',
 '<label>Proposed order <select id="orderId"><option value="42">42</option><option value="99">99</option></select></label><button id="approveCall">Approve shown call</button><button id="newCall">Create new call</button><div id="approvalWorld" class="world"></div>',
 "let callNo=1,receipt=null;function fingerprint(){return 'call_'+callNo+':cancelOrder:'+orderId.value;}function paintApproval(){const fp=fingerprint(),ok=receipt===fp;approvalWorld.innerHTML='<div class=\"row\"><div class=\"node\"><strong>proposal</strong><br>'+fp+'</div><div class=\"receipt\"><strong>approval receipt</strong><br>'+(receipt||'none')+'</div><div class=\"node '+(ok?'ok':'fail')+'\"><strong>'+(ok?'EXECUTE after guardrail':'BLOCKED')+'</strong><br>'+(ok?'exact call approved':'no matching decision')+'</div></div>';};approveCall.onclick=()=>{receipt=fingerprint();paintApproval();};newCall.onclick=()=>{callNo++;paintApproval();};orderId.onchange=paintApproval;paintApproval();",
 [q('A human approved call_A for order 42. Does call_B for order 99 inherit it?',['Yes, same tool name','No, it is a different call','Only if the model asks twice'],1,'Call-specific approval covers the inspected pending item.'),q('Why run an input guardrail again after approval?',['Risk or authorization may have changed while paused','To hide the arguments','To create a new user'],0,'Execution-time validation closes the stale-decision window.'),q('When is sticky always-approve safest?',['For every destructive tool','Only under explicit narrow policy where later calls have equivalent bounded risk','Whenever the user is offline'],1,'A tool name alone rarely captures argument-specific impact.')],
 [{'code':"proposal={'call_id':'call_A','tool':'cancelOrder','args':{'orderId':42}}",'note':'Capture the exact pending action the reviewer sees.'},{'code':"approved_call_id='call_A'",'note':'Store a decision for this call, not the tool name globally.'},{'code':"later={'call_id':'call_B','tool':'cancelOrder','args':{'orderId':99}}",'note':'A later model turn proposes a different effect.'},{'code':"may_execute=later['call_id']==approved_call_id",'note':'The later call fails closed and needs its own review.'}],{'may_execute':False},
 [['OpenAI Agents SDK human-in-the-loop guide','https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-js/guides/human-in-the-loop/','Living documentation checked October 19, 2026'],['OpenAI Agents SDK guardrails guide','https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-js/guides/guardrails/','Living documentation checked October 19, 2026'],['OpenAI Agents SDK context management','https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-js/guides/context/','Living documentation checked October 19, 2026']],
 'The receipt sits between proposal and side effect. Changing either call identity or arguments breaks the match visibly, so “approved tool” cannot be confused with “approved action.”',
 ['Which tool in your harness has the widest gap between harmless and dangerous arguments?','What state must be rechecked immediately before an approved action executes?']))

L.append(lesson(
 'technology-breakthroughs','Technology breakthroughs','Fan out light, apply weights in place, read many sums at once','Understand the FAST-ONN research mechanism without treating a lab benchmark as a shipped processor.','2026 research deep dive · optical computing',
 '<p>Imagine projecting one stencil onto many tiles at once instead of carrying each tile to one calculator. FAST-ONN fans out light from a dense laser array. A spatial light modulator stores many weights in the optical path. Detectors read many weighted sums in parallel.</p><p>“In-memory” here means weights are applied where the optical signal travels, reducing repeated movement between a separate weight store and arithmetic unit. Differential readout represents signed weights with paired positive and negative channels.</p>',
 '<p>A tiny teaching kernel has four inputs <code>[1,0,1,1]</code> and weights <code>[+1,-1,+1,0]</code>. The optical paths contribute <code>[1,0,1,0]</code>; the detector sum is 2. The paper reports a 5×5 VCSEL array and 3×3 parallel kernels at 100 mega-subimages/s, corresponding to 0.9 billion convolutions/s in its experiment.</p>',
 {'when':'Use this concept when evaluating research hardware for extremely high-throughput, low-latency convolution near sensors.','example':'An edge vision system must extract simple features from a very fast optical stream before sending a smaller representation onward.','decision':'Ask whether the demonstrated workload, accuracy, reprogramming, calibration, precision, size, power accounting, and system I/O match the intended product. Treat the published device as research evidence, not a drop-in GPU replacement.'},
 'Change one input bit. The parallel path contributions and detector sum update together.',
 'This exact integer dot product is a teaching model, not a simulation of diffraction, VCSEL noise, signed differential circuits, calibration, nonlinear layers, training, energy, or throughput. The paper’s 2026 benchmark is a specific experimental system and does not establish general production superiority.',
 '<label>Input x₂ <input id="optBit" type="range" min="0" max="1" value="0"></label><div id="opticWorld" class="world"></div>',
 "function paintOptic(){const x=[1,+optBit.value,1,1],w=[1,-1,1,0],c=x.map((v,i)=>v*w[i]),sum=c.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);opticWorld.innerHTML='<div class=\"optic\"><div class=\"node\"><strong>VCSEL inputs</strong><br>['+x.join(', ')+']</div><div><div class=beams>'+c.map(v=>'<div class=beam title=\"contribution '+v+'\"></div>').join('')+'</div><p><strong>SLM weights:</strong> ['+w.join(', ')+']<br><strong>path contributions:</strong> ['+c.join(', ')+']</p></div><div class=\"node ok\"><strong>detector sum</strong><br>'+sum+'</div></div>';};optBit.oninput=paintOptic;paintOptic();",
 [q('Where are weights applied in the described optical mechanism?',['In a spatial light modulator in the optical path','Only after cloud upload','In the quiz browser'],0,'The SLM supplies high-pixel-count in-memory weighting.'),q('Why use differential readout?',['To represent signed weights','To double every answer','To remove calibration'],0,'Paired channels let the optical system express positive and negative contributions.'),q('What does the reported 100 MFPS benchmark prove?',['Performance of the reported experiment under its setup','Universal production readiness','That every neural network runs faster'],0,'A lab benchmark is bounded by the demonstrated architecture, workload, and measurements.')],
 [{'code':'inputs=[1,0,1,1]\nweights=[1,-1,1,0]','note':'Create one tiny binary input and signed optical weight vector.'},{'code':'contributions=[x*w for x,w in zip(inputs,weights)]','note':'Each fan-out path applies its local weight in parallel conceptually.'},{'code':'detector_sum=sum(contributions)','note':'The detector combines the weighted contributions.'},{'code':"claim_scope='teaching dot product, not optical hardware'",'note':'Keep the model boundary explicit.'}],{'detector_sum':2},
 [['Liang et al., High-clockrate free-space optical in-memory computing','https://www.nature.com/articles/s41377-026-02206-8','Published February 13, 2026'],['Open-access paper PDF','https://www.nature.com/articles/s41377-026-02206-8.pdf','Version of record, February 13, 2026']],
 'The light paths show parallel multiplication before one detector sum. The changing contribution vector ties the familiar dot product to the optical layout without pretending the browser models the device physics.',
 ['Which system-level measurement would you require before comparing FAST-ONN with a GPU at the edge?','Which part of a full neural network pipeline is not represented by this convolution-only visual?']))

L.append(lesson(
 'ci-cd-github-actions','CI/CD & GitHub Actions','A skipped required workflow can wait forever','Keep the required check present even when expensive jobs are irrelevant to the changed paths.','Workflow structure · required checks',
 '<p>A building guard expects a stamped form. If the form office stays closed because your delivery is “documents only,” the guard does not interpret that as approval. The stamp never arrives.</p><p>GitHub behaves similarly: if path filtering skips an entire required workflow, its associated check can remain <strong>Pending</strong> and block the pull request. A skipped job, by contrast, reports Success. Keep a stable required gate job present on every relevant pull request and conditionally skip only the expensive work.</p>',
 '<p><code>ci</code> is required. A docs-only pull request does not match <code>paths: src/**</code>, so the entire workflow never runs and <code>ci</code> waits. Fix the shape: trigger the workflow for every pull request, detect changed paths inside it, run build only when needed, and have a small final <code>required-gate</code> job report success when work is irrelevant or verify all needed jobs when relevant.</p>',
 {'when':'Use this pattern when branch rules require a GitHub Actions check but some file changes do not need the expensive test/deploy jobs.','example':'Documentation-only changes should skip a 20-minute integration suite without leaving the required CI check pending.','decision':'Keep the required check name and workflow trigger stable. Put conditional logic at job/step level, and test docs-only, code-only, mixed, and merge-queue events.'},
 'Switch between workflow-level path filtering and an always-triggered gate. The PR check changes from Pending to Success.',
 'The browser models one required check and two change types. Real rulesets match exact check names and apps, path filters have diff limits, merge queues require merge_group, and needs/always() semantics matter. Test the actual repository rules and event payloads.',
 '<label>Change type <select id="changeType"><option value="docs">docs only</option><option value="code">source code</option></select></label><label>Design <select id="gateDesign"><option value="filter">filter whole workflow</option><option value="gate">always-triggered required gate</option></select></label><div id="checkWorld" class="world"></div>',
 "function paintCheck(){const docs=changeType.value==='docs',whole=gateDesign.value==='filter';let status=docs&&whole?'PENDING':docs?'SUCCESS · expensive job skipped':'SUCCESS · tests ran';checkWorld.innerHTML='<div class=\"row\"><div class=\"node\">PR: '+changeType.value+'</div><div class=\"node '+(docs&&whole?'fail':'ok')+'\">workflow '+(docs&&whole?'not created':'created')+'</div><div class=\"status '+(status.startsWith('PENDING')?'fail':'ok')+'\">required ci: '+status+'</div></div>';};changeType.onchange=paintCheck;gateDesign.onchange=paintCheck;paintCheck();",
 [q('Why can a workflow-level path filter block a PR?',['The required check is never reported and stays Pending','Docs always fail tests','GitHub deletes the branch'],0,'A skipped required workflow does not emit the successful check the rule expects.'),q('What should remain stable?',['The required gate check name and eligible trigger','The runner IP','Every log line'],0,'Rulesets need a predictable check producer.'),q('A merge queue requires this Actions check. Which event also matters?',['merge_group','issues','release only'],0,'GitHub documents merge_group as the merge-queue trigger for required checks.')],
 [{'code':"change='docs'\nworkflow_path_filter=True",'note':'A docs-only change does not match the whole-workflow filter.'},{'code':"workflow_created=not (change=='docs' and workflow_path_filter)",'note':'The filtered workflow is never created.'},{'code':"required_check='SUCCESS' if workflow_created else 'PENDING'",'note':'No workflow means no successful required check.'},{'code':"fixed_check='SUCCESS'  # always-triggered gate; expensive job may skip",'note':'A stable gate reports the deliberate no-op outcome.'}],{'fixed_check':'SUCCESS'},
 [['GitHub: troubleshooting required status checks','https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/how-tos/merge-and-close-pull-requests/troubleshooting-required-status-checks','Living documentation checked October 19, 2026'],['GitHub Actions workflow syntax: path filters','https://docs.github.com/actions/using-workflows/workflow-syntax-for-github-actions','Living documentation checked October 19, 2026'],['GitHub Actions events','https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/events-that-trigger-workflows','Living documentation checked October 19, 2026']],
 'The visual distinguishes three facts that logs often blur: whether a workflow exists, whether expensive tests run, and whether the required check receives a terminal status.',
 ['Which required checks in your repositories can be skipped by a workflow-level filter today?','How will you test the same gate under pull_request and merge_group?']))

L.append(lesson(
 'apis-microservices','APIs & Microservices','gRPC retries stop when response headers commit the RPC','Place retryable failures before the commit point and make side effects safe.','gRPC · resilience',
 '<p>A courier can try another route while the sealed delivery receipt is still blank. Once the recipient signs, the courier stops choosing routes and hands the outcome to you.</p><p>gRPC can buffer a call’s history and create a new attempt after eligible failures. But once the client receives response headers, the RPC is <strong>committed</strong>: gRPC stops automatic retry and gives control to the application.</p>',
 '<p>Attempt 1 sends <code>Reserve(order-42)</code>. If the transport closes with configured <code>UNAVAILABLE</code> before response headers, a policy may start attempt 2 after backoff. If headers arrive and the stream later fails, there is no automatic retry. The application must interpret the outcome. Because a server may have performed work before a failure is observed, use an idempotency key for side effects.</p>',
 {'when':'Use configured gRPC retries for transient failures on calls whose replay risk is understood and bounded.','example':'A reservation RPC briefly hits an unavailable replica before any response headers are returned.','decision':'Set a realistic deadline, retry only appropriate status codes with bounded attempts/backoff, throttle retry storms, and use idempotency keys or read-only semantics. Do not retry FAILED_PRECONDITION until state is fixed.'},
 'Move the failure before or after response headers. The attempt timeline shows whether gRPC may retry automatically.',
 'This browser ignores language-specific support, service-config delivery, pushback, throttling, jitter, hedging, streaming details, deadlines, and server-side execution races. It does not contact a gRPC server. Retry safety depends on method semantics and idempotency beyond the client library.',
 '<label>Failure point <select id="rpcFailure"><option value="before">before response headers</option><option value="after">after response headers</option></select></label><div id="retryWorld" class="world"></div>',
 "function paintRetry(){const before=rpcFailure.value==='before';retryWorld.innerHTML='<div class=attempts><div class=\"attempt fail\"><strong>attempt 1</strong><br>request sent<br>'+(before?'UNAVAILABLE before headers':'headers received, then failure')+'</div><div class=arrow>→</div><div class=\"attempt '+(before?'ok':'dim')+'\"><strong>attempt 2</strong><br>'+(before?'allowed by policy':'not created')+'</div></div><p><strong>Commit point:</strong> '+(before?'not reached; configured retry may proceed.':'headers already received; application owns the outcome.')+'</p>';};rpcFailure.onchange=paintRetry;paintRetry();",
 [q('When does gRPC consider the RPC committed for retry purposes?',['When response headers are received','When DNS resolves','When the client object is created'],0,'After response headers, no further automatic retries are attempted.'),q('UNAVAILABLE occurs before headers and matches a configured policy. What may happen?',['A new attempt after backoff','The method becomes read-only','Infinite retries by default'],0,'The client may replace the failed call within policy limits.'),q('Which status should not be retried until state is fixed?',['FAILED_PRECONDITION','UNAVAILABLE','OK'],0,'FAILED_PRECONDITION signals that explicit state repair is required first.')],
 [{'code':"failure_point='before_headers'\nretryable_status='UNAVAILABLE'",'note':'Attempt one fails before the commit point with an eligible status.'},{'code':"headers_received=failure_point=='after_headers'",'note':'No response headers have committed the RPC.'},{'code':"policy={'UNAVAILABLE':True,'max_attempts':3}",'note':'Apply a bounded explicit retry policy.'},{'code':"retry=policy.get(retryable_status,False) and not headers_received",'note':'The client may create a new attempt only before commitment.'}],{'retry':True},
 [['gRPC retry guide','https://grpc.io/docs/guides/retry/','Updated November 26, 2025'],['gRPC status codes guide','https://grpc.io/docs/guides/status-codes/','Updated August 21, 2024'],['gRPC deadlines guide','https://grpc.io/docs/guides/deadlines/','Updated July 7, 2025']],
 'The attempt timeline places the response-header commit point between “library may retry” and “application owns the outcome,” making a subtle protocol boundary visible.',
 ['Which of your RPC methods are safe to replay, and what idempotency key protects side effects?','Which metrics distinguish original attempts, retry attempts, and application-level retries?']))

